1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,475
Before I wrote any code for this hotel group,

2
00:00:06,325 --> 00:00:08,458
I spent a day just asking questions.

3
00:00:09,308 --> 00:00:11,377
This repo is what came out of that.

4
00:00:12,227 --> 00:00:15,150
It's a simulated client, with synthetic data.

5
00:00:15,766 --> 00:00:17,259
I talked to six people.

6
00:00:18,109 --> 00:00:22,973
The general manager, the front-desk lead, activities, revenue, IT, and
a guest.

7
00:00:23,823 --> 00:00:29,562
The front-desk lead told me a guest waited four minutes while they
radioed a manager about one fee.

8
00:00:30,412 --> 00:00:32,524
That one answer shaped the first stage.

9
00:00:33,141 --> 00:00:34,634
I scored six ideas,

10
00:00:35,484 --> 00:00:37,191
then ran a four-check test.

11
00:00:38,041 --> 00:00:44,142
Has someone felt it, would someone pay, can I build a first version in
a week, and do I know the first user.

12
00:00:44,992 --> 00:00:46,592
Automatic refunds failed.

13
00:00:47,442 --> 00:00:50,877
The manager said that's exactly what would make them switch it off.

14
00:00:51,485 --> 00:00:52,680
Here's the pricing.

15
00:00:53,530 --> 00:00:56,111
The only measured input is the model cost:

16
00:00:56,961 --> 00:01:02,230
eighteen cents per thousand questions, and about a dollar twenty-four
per thousand emails.

17
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:04,915
Everything else is an assumption,

18
00:01:05,765 --> 00:01:07,600
so it's a range, not a promise.

19
00:01:08,203 --> 00:01:09,675
The point is the order.

20
00:01:10,525 --> 00:01:12,104
Staff-facing before guests,

21
00:01:12,954 --> 00:01:15,172
read-only before anything that writes.

22
00:01:16,022 --> 00:01:19,734
Every later stage links back here, and the links are in the README.
